  "resultText": "A clinic cancel with `{ \"reason\": \"clinic\", \"refund\": true }` on an appointment already charged a no-show fee now refunds the no-show invoice. `npm test` passes (35 unit tests and 6 e2e tests), and typecheck and lint are clean.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Scheduling:**\n  - A no-show appointment can now be cancelled, but only when the reason is `clinic` and `refund` is `true`. Any other cancel of a no-show is still rejected with 422.\n  - That cancel publishes the usual `AppointmentCancelled`, which front-desk uses to close the visit. It also publishes a new `NoShowFeeRefundRequested` event, which carries only `appointmentId` and `personId`.\n  - The new event name is a constant in `src/contracts/scheduling.events.ts`, as R10 requires.\n- **Billing:**\n  - A new listener handles the refund event and sets the no-show invoice's status to `REFUNDED`.\n  - If the appointment has no no-show invoice, or the invoice is already refunded, the handler does nothing.\n  - `PrismaInvoiceRepository.save` now upserts, so it can update an existing invoice.\n\n**Context map:** I didn't create `context-map.json`. The feature uses the existing `scheduling → billing` relation, and no new context-to-context relation is needed. I used a dedicated event rather than adding refund data to `AppointmentCancelled`, because front-desk would receive data it doesn't need (R4).\n\n**Tests:** I added unit tests for the appointment, the scheduling service, the invoice and the billing service. I also added e2e tests for the full refund flow and for a plain cancel of a no-show being rejected with the invoice left open.",
